\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces  \textcolor {red}{Albert: {Todo}} Performance of {$\mathbb  {B}\text  {it}\mathbb  {Z}$}\ on different computations that are costly to arithmetize and prove with standard proof systems. For each such computation, we also benchmark the performance of prior state of the art protocols. All experiments were run on a MacBook Air M5 24\,GB, multi-threaded, targeting $100$ bits of security (Binius64 is at 96 bits). See \cref  {s:experiments} for details on these and more experiments.  We mostly display $2^{17}$ multiplications because this is roughly the amount where Binius64 achieves its highest throughput. For different amounts, {$\mathbb  {B}\text  {it}\mathbb  {Z}$}\ outperforms by a wider margin. For larger sizes Binius64 runs out of memory, as showcased in the $2^{21}$ multiplications row. }}{4}{table.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
