\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Witness bit-size per SHA-256 compression as per two different arithmetizations. The $\mathsf  {CM}$ row indicates the bit-size when arithmetizing SHA-256 via an instance of the $\mathsf  {CM}$ relation (see \eqref  {e:CM_rel}) using the methods from \cref  {e:CM_rel,r:CM_is_NP_complete}, see \url  {https://github.com/reilabs/freigen/blob/324935e/Freigen/Circuit/Examples/Sha256.lean} \textcolor {red}{Albert: {Change this to avoid deanonymization in the EC submission}}. The second row displays the witness bit-size in the arithmetization from Binius64 \cite  {binius64}, which is an R1CS constraint over a binary field. Flock's \cite  {flock} arithmetization has 31400 bits per compression. The AI row refers to an optimized arithmetization found by an AI working against a Lean specification in the zkGolf competition \url  {https://zk.golf/}. }}{8}{table.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:sha_arithmetization}{{2}{8}{Witness bit-size per SHA-256 compression as per two different arithmetizations. The $\mathsf {CM}$ row indicates the bit-size when arithmetizing SHA-256 via an instance of the $\mathsf {CM}$ relation (see \eqref {e:CM_rel}) using the methods from \cref {e:CM_rel,r:CM_is_NP_complete}, see \url {https://github.com/reilabs/freigen/blob/324935e/Freigen/Circuit/Examples/Sha256.lean} \albertimportant {Change this to avoid deanonymization in the EC submission}. The second row displays the witness bit-size in the arithmetization from Binius64 \cite {binius64}, which is an R1CS constraint over a binary field. Flock's \cite {flock} arithmetization has 31400 bits per compression. The AI row refers to an optimized arithmetization found by an AI working against a Lean specification in the zkGolf competition \url {https://zk.golf/}}{table.caption.4}{}}
